  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Ketoprofen may interact with Neutral alpha-glucosidase C through multiple indirect pathways. The drug is known to target Solute carrier family 22 member 6, which is a target of several compounds, including 2-{4-[4-({4-[2-methyl-1-(1-methylethyl)-1H-imidazol-5-yl]pyrimidin-2-yl}amino)phenyl]piperazin-1-yl}-2-oxoethanol, which in turn targets Neutral alpha-glucosidase C. Additionally, Ketoprofen is also associated with P-glycoprotein 1, which is a target of lafutidine, and lafutidine further targets Neutral alpha-glucosidase C. These findings indicate that Ketoprofen may exert its effects through different molecular mechanisms, potentially influencing the activity of Neutral alpha-glucosidase C in various cellular contexts. The evidence suggests a possible role for Ketoprofen in modulating the activity of Neutral alpha-glucosidase C, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
